<!-- ===================================================================
     06_thrust_craft.xml  -  a craft with steerable thrust vectors
     ===================================================================
     An abstract 12 kg test craft with five thrusters. This is the scene
     the thrust/control material is built around.

     HOW A THRUSTER IS DEFINED. Two elements working together:

       <site>     WHERE the thrust is applied, and the frame it is
                  applied in. A site is just a named point + orientation
                  on a body. It has no mass and no collision.

       <general>  the actuator that pushes on that site. Its `gear`
                  attribute is the force direction IN THE SITE FRAME:
                      gear = "Fx Fy Fz Tx Ty Tz"
                  The first three are force, the last three torque.

     The split that matters for control:

       data.ctrl[i]                  -> MAGNITUDE, in Newtons
       model.actuator_gear[i][:3]    -> DIRECTION, as a unit vector

     Both are writable at runtime, every step. Writing ctrl throttles the
     thruster; writing gear STEERS it (a gimbal). That is the whole
     mechanism - see scripts/thrusters.py for the wrapper around it.

     *** THE TRAP ***  gear SCALES the force, it does not merely aim it.
     gear=[0,0,2] with ctrl=10 gives 20 N, not 10 N. A direction vector
     must be NORMALIZED. Thruster.set() does this for you; if you write
     actuator_gear by hand, normalize it yourself.

     FRAMES. gear is in the site frame, which rides the body - so a
     thruster keeps pointing "down the craft's axis" no matter how the
     craft tumbles, exactly like a real engine. You do not have to
     rotate anything by hand.

     CONVENTIONS (matching assignment1/.../base_frame.py and the course
     material): Z is up, the main thrust axis is at X = Y = 0, and
     everything here is SI - metres, kilograms, seconds, Newtons,
     radians. Note the CAD model thrusts in -Z because that test stand
     fires DOWN into a bench; a flight vehicle thrusts +Z, as here.

     Run it:
       uv run python scripts/fly.py scenes/06_thrust_craft.xml \
           --controller example_hover:hover --arrows
     =================================================================== -->

<mujoco model="thrust_craft">

  <option timestep="0.002" gravity="0 0 -1.62"/>
  <!--                             ^^^^^ lunar surface gravity, m/s^2.
       Use -9.81 for Earth. The craft below masses 12 kg, so hovering
       needs m*g = 19.44 N here but 117.7 N on Earth - more than the
       main thruster's 60 N limit. Gravity choice and thrust sizing are
       the same decision. -->

  <!-- Offscreen framebuffer for headless rendering; the 640x480 default
       is a hard error if you ask Renderer for anything bigger. -->
  <visual>
    <global offwidth="1280" offheight="720"/>
  </visual>

  <asset>
    <texture name="grid" type="2d" builtin="checker"
             rgb1="0.2 0.3 0.4" rgb2="0.1 0.15 0.2" width="300" height="300"/>
    <material name="grid_mat" texture="grid" texrepeat="10 10" reflectance="0.1"/>
  </asset>

  <default>
    <!-- Sites are visual markers only - no mass, no collision. Drawn
         small and orange so the nozzles are visible in the viewer. -->
    <site type="sphere" size="0.025" rgba="1.0 0.55 0.1 1"/>
  </default>

  <worldbody>
    <light pos="0 0 6" dir="0 0 -1" diffuse="0.8 0.8 0.8"/>
    <geom name="floor" type="plane" size="20 20 0.1" material="grid_mat"/>

    <body name="craft" pos="0 0 2.0">
      <freejoint/>

      <!-- The hull. 12 kg explicit, rather than letting MuJoCo infer it
           from a density, because thrust sizing is all about m*g and a
           made-up density would make the numbers meaningless.
           0.15 x 0.15 x 0.25 half-extents => a 0.3 x 0.3 x 0.5 m body. -->
      <geom name="hull" type="box" size="0.15 0.15 0.25" mass="12"
            rgba="0.75 0.78 0.82 1"/>

      <!-- ===========================================================
           MAIN ENGINE. On the thrust axis (X = Y = 0), at the base.
           ===========================================================
           pos is where the nozzle sits; -0.25 is the bottom face.
           Because X = Y = 0, thrust along +Z passes through the centre
           of mass and produces NO torque - pure translation. Steer it
           off-axis with Thruster.set(direction=...) and it starts
           producing torque too, which is thrust vectoring. -->
      <site name="noz_main" pos="0 0 -0.25" size="0.04"/>

      <!-- ===========================================================
           RCS QUAD. Above the centre of mass, firing SIDEWAYS.
           ===========================================================
           These exist to make torque. A force applied at a point offset
           from the centre of mass produces a moment r x F, so an offset
           site lets you rotate the craft.

           Note the site `euler` values: each nozzle is rotated so that
           its +Z (the direction `gear="0 0 1"` means) points HORIZONTALLY
           outward. That matters. An earlier version of this scene had all
           four firing +Z like the main engine, which meant every attitude
           correction also shoved the craft upward by 5 N - a parasitic
           lift that fights the altitude loop. Firing sideways from a
           point 0.25 m above the CoM gives almost pure torque.

           euler="0 90 0"  rotates nozzle +Z to world +X
           euler="0 -90 0" rotates nozzle +Z to world -X
           euler="-90 0 0" rotates nozzle +Z to world +Y
           euler="90 0 0"  rotates nozzle +Z to world -Y

           Fire an opposing PAIR together and the torques cancel while the
           side forces add - that is translation. Fire one alone and you
           get rotation. See example_hover.vectored_translate. -->
      <site name="noz_rcs_px" pos=" 0.15 0 0.25" size="0.02" euler="0  90 0"/>
      <site name="noz_rcs_nx" pos="-0.15 0 0.25" size="0.02" euler="0 -90 0"/>
      <site name="noz_rcs_py" pos="0  0.15 0.25" size="0.02" euler="-90 0 0"/>
      <site name="noz_rcs_ny" pos="0 -0.15 0.25" size="0.02" euler=" 90 0 0"/>

      <!-- A marker at the centre of mass, for reference when watching
           torques. Visual only. -->
      <site name="com" pos="0 0 0" size="0.03" rgba="0.3 0.9 0.4 0.5"/>

      <!-- A chase camera riding the craft, so headless renders frame the
           vehicle instead of the whole world. The free camera zooms out
           to fit the scene, which makes the thrust arrows too small to
           see. Select it with `--camera chase`, or press ']' in the
           viewer. mode="trackcom" keeps it pointed at the centre of
           mass while the craft moves. -->
      <camera name="chase" pos="2.8 -3.2 0.15"
              xyaxes="0.75 0.66 0 -0.04 0.05 1.0" mode="trackcom"/>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <!-- ===============================================================
       ACTUATORS. One <general> per thruster, each bound to its site.
       ===============================================================
       gear="0 0 1 0 0 0"  => +Z in the site frame, i.e. the craft
                              pushes itself "up" along its own axis.
       ctrlrange="0 60"    => 0 to 60 N. MuJoCo CLAMPS ctrl to this
                              range automatically (verified: ctrl=999
                              gives 60 N, ctrl=-5 gives 0 N). The lower
                              bound is 0 because a rocket cannot pull.

       Names are prefixed thr_ so ThrusterSet can discover them and
       strip the prefix: thr_main -> thrusters["main"].
       =============================================================== -->
  <actuator>
    <!-- Main engine: 60 N, comfortably above the 19.44 N lunar hover
         requirement so there is control authority to climb. -->
    <general name="thr_main" site="noz_main" gear="0 0 1 0 0 0"
             ctrlrange="0 60"/>

    <!-- RCS: 20 N each. Their job is attitude, not lift, but they still
         have to OUT-TORQUE the main engine's gimbal disturbance.
         Sizing, which is a real design calculation and not a guess:
           main engine 60 N max, nozzle 0.25 m below the CoM, cant
           limited to ~37 deg  =>  up to 60*sin(37) * 0.25 = 9.0 N*m of
           disturbance; at the 24 N hover-ish throttle used by
           vectored_translate it is 3.6 N*m (measured).
           one RCS at 20 N on a 0.25 m arm = 5.0 N*m.
         At 5 N each (1.25 N*m) the attitude loop LOSES to the gimbal and
         the craft tumbles to a stable upside-down hover - position
         converges while tilt sits at 180 deg. No amount of gain tuning
         fixes an under-actuated vehicle; the thrusters have to be big
         enough. This is the lesson, and it is why the number is 20. -->
    <general name="thr_rcs_px" site="noz_rcs_px" gear="0 0 1 0 0 0"
             ctrlrange="0 20"/>
    <general name="thr_rcs_nx" site="noz_rcs_nx" gear="0 0 1 0 0 0"
             ctrlrange="0 20"/>
    <general name="thr_rcs_py" site="noz_rcs_py" gear="0 0 1 0 0 0"
             ctrlrange="0 20"/>
    <general name="thr_rcs_ny" site="noz_rcs_ny" gear="0 0 1 0 0 0"
             ctrlrange="0 20"/>
  </actuator>

  <!-- ===============================================================
       SENSORS. The "flight computer's" view of the state.
       ===============================================================
       These land in data.sensordata as one flat float array, in the
       order declared here: pos(3) vel(3) quat(4) angvel(3) = 13 floats.

       You do NOT need sensors to write a controller - data.xpos,
       data.qvel and data.xquat are readable directly, which is what
       CraftState does. Sensors matter when you want to model what a
       real vehicle can actually measure: add noise, bias or a slower
       update rate here and your controller has to cope, exactly as it
       would on the bench.
       =============================================================== -->
  <sensor>
    <framepos     objtype="body" objname="craft" name="craft_pos"/>
    <framelinvel  objtype="body" objname="craft" name="craft_vel"/>
    <framequat    objtype="body" objname="craft" name="craft_quat"/>
    <frameangvel  objtype="body" objname="craft" name="craft_angvel"/>
  </sensor>
</mujoco>
